WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.301
Since the first X-Men movie all the way back
in 2000, the architects of the franchise have

00:00:05.301 --> 00:00:09.440
been dropping in small nods and references
to the comics that inspired the films.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.820
Dark Phoenix continues the tradition with
some big and not-so-big Easter eggs for the

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.280
eagle eyed and comics versed.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.800
Here are some you may have blinked and missed
on your first watch.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.820
And if you haven't seen the movie, here's
your warning: Spoilers are ahead.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.040
Dark Phoenix opens with a tragic scene set
in 1975, a pivotal year for the X-Men with

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.750
the release of Giant-Size X-Men #1, an issue
that single-handedly revitalized a flagging

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.370
Marvel franchise by bringing on characters
including Nightcrawler, Colossus and Wolverine,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.000
a guy you might have heard of.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:41.900
"Who are you?"

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:43.980
"You know who I am."

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.100
Before the scene turns tragic and the Greys
crash their car, an argument over what's on

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.040
the radio leads to two significant songs being
played as Jean telekinetically turns the dial.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:59.600
The first is the 1967 Glen Campbell version
of "By the Time I Get To Phoenix," which,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.640
uh, should be pretty self-explanatory.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.780
The other is a little deeper in its meaning.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.580
It's "Werewolves of London" by Warren Zevon,
which was released three years later in 1978

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.840
making it an anachronism .

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.940
It'd seem to be a reference to the first issue
of Excalibur, titled "Warwolves of London."

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.520
That series saw a ragtag team of former X-Men,
including the Rachel Summers version of Phoenix

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.280
and Nightcrawler, carrying on in the United
Kingdom after they believed the rest of the

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.180
team to be dead.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:31.160
Excalibur also stars Kitty Pryde, who seemingly
makes a very quick cameo phasing through a

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.340
wall late in the movie.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.260
The movie's "present day" setting, as it were,
is 1992, the year the popular X-Men cartoon

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.260
series started.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.780
But while the early '90s weren't exactly associated
with disco, we still get an appearance from

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.100
the disco-dancing mutant, Dazzler.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.560
Dazzler belts out some dance tunes at the
outdoor party celebrating the mission to save

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.789
the NASA astronauts from certain doom on the
space shuttle Endeavour, which notably did

00:01:54.789 --> 00:01:56.990
have its first mission in May 1992.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:01.270
Though she's a superhero herself in the comics,
Dazzler doesn't seem to be much more than

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.380
the X-Men's personal party singer here.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.780
Speaking of very specific jobs, the party
scene also includes a quick appearance by

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.980
recent X-Men recruit Match, who lights a fire.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.340
Like Phoenix, he's got a bit of a temper.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.940
It wouldn't be too surprising if you missed
the name of the alien race that served as

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.400
Dark Phoenix's ultimate antagonists, since
it's only mentioned once in the whole movie.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.500
They're the D'Bari, a race of aliens who,
in the comics, look kind of like asparagus.

00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:31.901
They've appeared in a number of comics stories
over the years, first in Avengers #4, and

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:32.960
sometimes as villains.

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:37.719
But for the purposes of Dark Phoenix, their
most important appearance was in Uncanny X-Men

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:42.460
#135, in which a Phoenix-possessed Jean Grey
destroys their home planet.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.840
That association with the Phoenix Force is
basically where the movie D'Bari's similarities

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.250
to the comic characters end.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.780
Their shapeshifting powers in the film are
much more like Skrulls.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:56.680
Jessica Chastain plays the D'Bari leader,
who is also very different in the comics.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.560
"They can't begin to comprehend what you are.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:01.740
Even your X-men."

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:07.060
Instead of desiring the Phoenix Force for
the purposes of creating new worlds, the D'Bari

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:12.180
leader in comics, Vuk, simply wants to attack
Jean Grey and get revenge for the destruction

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:12.980
of his home planet.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.840
He gets a pretty sweet suit of armor and adopts
the name Starhammer in one attempt, but is

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.880
not successful.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:24.640
Time traveler, professional rebel, part-time
sleuth and all-around cool guy Lucas Bishop

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.359
actually appeared in one X-Men movie: 2014's
Days of Future Past.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.320
He died in that movie, but then his death
got erased when the timeline changed at the

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.200
end of that movie.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.140
He only manages to return in Dark Phoenix
in the name of the Red Hook-area electrical

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:39.640
utility.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.840
In the scene where the X-Men arrive to confront
Jean in the neighborhood where she grew up

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.459
after she discovers her father is still alive,
a truck emblazoned with the words "Bishop

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.900
Power" is visible in the background of several
shots.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.480
That may be a nod to Bishop's mutant abilities.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:58.180
While they're not specifically electrical
in nature, he can absorb and release various

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.100
forms of energy.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:00.200
Or who knows?

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:04.780
Maybe the movie version of Lucas Bishop's
family decided to found a public utility,

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:07.200
and the future version of him will just run
that.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.380
It's good work if you can get it.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.920
The Magneto of Dark Phoenix is more or less
retired.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.900
He tells Jean that he has essentially decided
that hurting people won't do him any good

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:20.510
anymore, so he's stopped with all that, giving
up his violent ways from the previous films

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.640
and opting to live on a secluded island that's
a haven for mutants who just want to get away

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:25.180
from it all.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:29.900
It's a simple community built from old shipping
containers and other junk Magneto has likely

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.760
pulled out of the ocean with his powers.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.920
"This land was given to us by the U.S. government."

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.660
"We have no intention of taking it back.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:37.860
We're not here for you."

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:42.340
Magneto's new homeland isn't given a name
in the movie, but it seems to be a version

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.740
of Genosha, the island mutant homeland from
the comics.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:49.460
The comics version, however, is a much more
imposing place.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:54.220
Initially, it was a nation where mutants were
enslaved, but the X-Men toppled its regime

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:55.940
while saving allies imprisoned there.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.900
Later, the United Nations gave control of
the country to Magneto, who made it a mutants-only

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:01.400
nation.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.560
It's been the site of numerous conflicts since
then.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.340
There are not many jokes in Dark Phoenix.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.580
There are almost zero comedic moments, despite
earlier movies in the franchise having a good

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:11.580
bit of comic relief.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.720
Remember when Wolverine verified that he wasn't
Mystique when Cyclops asked him about it in

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:17.480
the first movie?

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:18.960
"Hey, hey!

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:20.000
It's me."

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:21.300
"Prove it."

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:22.680
"You're a d---."

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:24.920
"Okay."

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.720
Dark Phoenix totally plays it straight, but
there's one thing you could definitely call

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:34.640
a sort of meta-joke in the new universe where
Disney has merged 20th Century Fox.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:39.360
As some viewers noticed in the trailers, the
paramilitary group that apprehends the X-Men

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.520
near the end of the movie goes by the initials
"MCU," according to the patches on their sleeves.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.440
It likely stands for something like "Mutant
Control Unit."

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:52.160
In the cartoon, this organization is called
the Mutant Control Agency, but it's clear

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.680
the filmmakers wanted to change that one letter
to have a little fun.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:06:01.100
A heavily militarized group using futuristic
weapons and going by the initials MCU take

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:06.180
these 20th Century Fox versions of mutants
away to reprogram and bend them to their will.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.260
That sure seems like some kind of metaphor
for something.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.000
But also, they're not all bad.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.080
When the real bad guys attack, they team up
with the X-Men.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.160
So that's nice.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.580
Following Jean's sacrifice at the end of the
climactic battle, we see that Charles Xavier

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.620
has moved on to live a life of playing chess
outside of cafes.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:28.470
Hank McCoy, now staying in his Beast form,
has taken over as headmaster of the school,

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:32.420
and he's given it a new name: The Jean Grey
School for Gifted Youngsters.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:37.360
It's his tribute to her for choosing the path
of light, and a not-so-subtle jab at his old

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:37.860
mentor.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.500
The school has borne Jean's name in the comics,
too.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.640
In the series Wolverine and the X-Men, new
headmasters Wolverine and Kitty Pryde rename

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:49.770
the school The Jean Grey School of Higher
Learning after Professor X's death, one of

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:50.920
many he has endured.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.711
In that instance, it's less a slight toward
Xavier and more of an indication of a new

00:06:55.711 --> 00:07:00.610
generation of students at the school, which
would, of course, bear Xavier's name again

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:01.380
a few years later.

